### What is `Collectors.groupingBy`?

`groupingBy` is a collector that partitions the elements of a stream into groups keyed by a classifier function, returning a `Map` from each key to the elements that fall under it. It is the stream equivalent of a SQL `GROUP BY`.

```java
package com.bank.report;

import com.bank.model.Employee;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import static java.util.stream.Collectors.groupingBy;

public class GroupingDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> employees = List.of(
                new Employee(1, "Rajesh", "Trading", new BigDecimal("1200000")),
                new Employee(2, "Priya", "Risk", new BigDecimal("850000")),
                new Employee(3, "Pankaj", "Management", new BigDecimal("2000000"))
        );
        Map<String, List<Employee>> byDept = employees.stream()
                .collect(groupingBy(Employee::department));
        System.out.println(byDept.keySet());
    }
}
```

```text
[Risk, Trading, Management]   // HashMap: order is unspecified
```

The `Employee` record:

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public record Employee(long id, String name, String department, BigDecimal salary) { }
```

### What are the three overloads of `groupingBy`?

`groupingBy` has three forms that build on each other. The one-argument form groups into lists in a `HashMap`. The two-argument form adds a downstream collector. The three-argument form inserts a map factory between the classifier and the downstream.

| Overload | Signature shape | Result | Key order |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1-arg | `groupingBy(classifier)` | `Map<K, List<T>>` | none (HashMap) |
| 2-arg | `groupingBy(classifier, downstream)` | `Map<K, D>` | none (HashMap) |
| 3-arg | `groupingBy(classifier, mapFactory, downstream)` | `M extends Map<K, D>` | decided by factory |

```java
import static java.util.stream.Collectors.counting;
import static java.util.stream.Collectors.groupingBy;

// 1-arg: Map<String, List<Employee>> in a HashMap
Map<String, List<Employee>> a = employees.stream()
        .collect(groupingBy(Employee::department));

// 2-arg: Map<String, Long> count per department, still a HashMap
Map<String, Long> b = employees.stream()
        .collect(groupingBy(Employee::department, counting()));
```

### How do you group into a sorted `TreeMap`?

Pass `TreeMap::new` as the map factory in the three-argument form. The resulting `TreeMap` keeps keys in natural order, so departments come out alphabetically with no extra sort step.

```java
package com.bank.report;

import com.bank.model.Employee;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.TreeMap;
import static java.util.stream.Collectors.groupingBy;
import static java.util.stream.Collectors.toList;

public class SortedGrouping {
    public TreeMap<String, List<Employee>> byDepartmentSorted(List<Employee> employees) {
        return employees.stream()
                .collect(groupingBy(Employee::department, TreeMap::new, toList()));
    }
}
```

```text
{Management=[...], Risk=[...], Trading=[...]}   // TreeMap: alphabetical keys
```

### How do you choose the downstream collector?

The downstream collector decides what each group's value becomes. `toList()` keeps the elements. `counting()` gives a count. `mapping(...)` transforms each element before collecting. `averagingDouble(...)` or `summingDouble(...)` aggregate numbers. You can combine a sorted key map with any of these.

```java
import static java.util.stream.Collectors.groupingBy;
import static java.util.stream.Collectors.mapping;
import static java.util.stream.Collectors.toList;
import static java.util.stream.Collectors.counting;

// Sorted keys, values are employee names only
TreeMap<String, List<String>> names = employees.stream()
        .collect(groupingBy(Employee::department, TreeMap::new,
                mapping(Employee::name, toList())));

// Sorted keys, values are head counts
TreeMap<String, Long> headcount = employees.stream()
        .collect(groupingBy(Employee::department, TreeMap::new, counting()));
```

```text
names     = {Management=[Pankaj], Risk=[Priya], Trading=[Rajesh]}
headcount = {Management=1, Risk=1, Trading=1}
```

### How do you sort keys with a custom comparator?

A bare `TreeMap::new` uses natural order. For a custom order, supply a lambda that builds a `TreeMap` with your comparator, for example descending order or case-insensitive order.

```java
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.TreeMap;
import static java.util.stream.Collectors.groupingBy;
import static java.util.stream.Collectors.toList;

// Departments in reverse alphabetical order
TreeMap<String, List<Employee>> reverse = employees.stream()
        .collect(groupingBy(Employee::department,
                () -> new TreeMap<>(Comparator.reverseOrder()),
                toList()));
```

```text
{Trading=[...], Risk=[...], Management=[...]}
```

### How do the map-factory choices compare?

| Map factory | Key order | Typical use |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `HashMap::new` (default) | unspecified | best throughput, order irrelevant |
| `TreeMap::new` | natural sort order | alphabetical or numeric key listing |
| `() -> new TreeMap<>(cmp)` | custom sort order | reverse or case-insensitive keys |
| `LinkedHashMap::new` | first-seen insertion order | preserve the order keys appeared |

```java
import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import static java.util.stream.Collectors.groupingBy;
import static java.util.stream.Collectors.toList;

// Preserve the order in which departments first appeared in the stream
LinkedHashMap<String, List<Employee>> insertionOrder = employees.stream()
        .collect(groupingBy(Employee::department, LinkedHashMap::new, toList()));
```

### What are common mistakes?

The first mistake is grouping into a `HashMap` and then being surprised the keys are unordered, or sorting the entries afterward when a `TreeMap` factory would have done it in one step.

```java
// WRONG style: group then sort the keys separately
Map<String, List<Employee>> hash = employees.stream()
        .collect(groupingBy(Employee::department));
List<String> sortedKeys = hash.keySet().stream().sorted().toList(); // extra pass
```

```java
// CORRECT: sorted keys straight from the collector
TreeMap<String, List<Employee>> sorted = employees.stream()
        .collect(groupingBy(Employee::department, TreeMap::new, toList()));
```

The second mistake is a classifier that returns `null`, which a `TreeMap` rejects with a `NullPointerException` because it cannot order a null key, even though a `HashMap` would have allowed one null key.

```java
// WRONG: null department becomes a null key -> TreeMap throws NPE
employees.stream().collect(groupingBy(Employee::department, TreeMap::new, toList()));
```

```java
// CORRECT: normalize the key so there is no null
employees.stream().collect(groupingBy(
        e -> e.department() == null ? "UNKNOWN" : e.department(),
        TreeMap::new, toList()));
```

### What are edge cases and senior nuances?

A senior point is that the map factory is a deliberate design choice, not a detail. `TreeMap` gives sorted keys and accepts a custom comparator, `LinkedHashMap` preserves first-seen order, and `HashMap` gives the best throughput when order does not matter. Because the downstream collector is fully composable, you can nest collectors to build richer values, for example a sorted map of department to the average salary.

```java
import java.util.TreeMap;
import static java.util.stream.Collectors.averagingDouble;
import static java.util.stream.Collectors.groupingBy;

// Sorted department -> average salary
TreeMap<String, Double> avgByDept = employees.stream()
        .collect(groupingBy(Employee::department, TreeMap::new,
                averagingDouble(e -> e.salary().doubleValue())));
```

Another nuance is that `groupingBy` is not concurrent; for a large parallel stream use `groupingByConcurrent` with a `ConcurrentHashMap`, but note that drops key ordering, so you would sort afterward if you also needed sorted keys.

### When should you use the three-argument form?

Use the three-argument `groupingBy` whenever you care about the type or ordering of the result map, or need a non-list value per group. Reach for `TreeMap::new` when a report must display groups in a stable, sorted order on every load, which is exactly what an org-chart or statement screen needs. Stick with the one-argument form when order is irrelevant and lists per key are enough.
